  {
   "xml_index": 1,
   "xml_name": "L08_001_Create A Tuple",
   "display_name": "Create A Tuple",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Welcome to Lab 8! A <b>tuple</b> is an ordered sequence of values, very much like a list, but written with <b>round brackets</b> <code>( )</code> instead of square brackets:</p>\n<pre>point = (3, 7)\ncolours = (&quot;red&quot;, &quot;green&quot;, &quot;blue&quot;)</pre>\n<p>Tuples have indexes just like lists, starting at 0, and <code>len()</code> works the same way:</p>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-1</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">colours =</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(31,95,153);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;red&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(52,129,188);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;green&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(74,163,223);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;blue&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">1</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">2</td></tr></table>\n<p>The big difference, which this lab is about: a tuple is <b>immutable</b>, it can <b>never be changed</b> after it is made. We will see why that is useful.</p>\n<pre>point = (3, 7)\ncolours = (&quot;red&quot;, &quot;green&quot;, &quot;blue&quot;)\nprint(point)\nprint(colours[0], colours[-1], len(colours))\nprint(type(point))</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>(3, 7)\nred blue 3\n&lt;class &#x27;tuple&#x27;&gt;</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>make_point(x, y)</code>, which returns the tuple <code>(x, y)</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>make_point(3, 7)</code> returns <code>(3, 7)</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def make_point(x, y):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(make_point(3, 7))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
