  {
   "xml_index": 2,
   "xml_name": "L08_002_The One Item Tuple",
   "display_name": "The One Item Tuple",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>The <b>comma</b> makes a tuple, not the brackets. This matters for a tuple with only one item:</p>\n<ul><li><code>(4)</code> is just the number 4 in brackets (like <code>(2 + 2)</code>);</li><li><code>(4,)</code> is a tuple containing 4. The trailing comma is needed.</li></ul>\n<p>The empty tuple is <code>()</code>.</p>\n<pre>a = (4)\nb = (4,)\nprint(a, type(a))\nprint(b, type(b), len(b))\nprint((), len(()))</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>4 &lt;class &#x27;int&#x27;&gt;\n(4,) &lt;class &#x27;tuple&#x27;&gt; 1\n() 0</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>wrap(x)</code>, which returns a tuple containing only <code>x</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>wrap(4)</code> returns <code>(4,)</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def wrap(x):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(wrap(4))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
