   "xml_index": 3,
   "xml_name": "L08_003_Converting With tuple",
   "display_name": "Converting With tuple",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><code>tuple(...)</code> converts other sequences into a tuple, just like <code>list(...)</code> makes a list:</p>\n<pre>print(tuple([1, 2, 3]))\nprint(tuple(&quot;abc&quot;))\nprint(tuple(range(4)))\nprint(list((7, 8)))</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>(1, 2, 3)\n(&#x27;a&#x27;, &#x27;b&#x27;, &#x27;c&#x27;)\n(0, 1, 2, 3)\n[7, 8]</pre>\n<p>The last line goes the other way: <code>list(...)</code> turns a tuple into a list.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write two functions:</p>\n<ul><li><code>as_tuple(L)</code> returns a tuple with the same items as the list <code>L</code>;</li><li><code>chars(word)</code> returns a tuple of the characters of the string <code>word</code>.</li></ul>\n<p>For example, <code>as_tuple([1, 2, 3])</code> returns <code>(1, 2, 3)</code> and <code>chars(\"hi\")</code> returns <code>('h', 'i')</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def as_tuple(L):\n    # write your code here\n\n\ndef chars(word):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(as_tuple([1, 2, 3]))\n# print(chars(\"hi\"))\n",
