  {
   "xml_index": 5,
   "xml_name": "L08_005_Packing And Unpacking",
   "display_name": "Packing And Unpacking",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Writing several values separated by commas <b>packs</b> them into a tuple (the brackets are optional). The opposite, <b>unpacking</b>, puts the items of a tuple into separate variables in one line. The number of variables must match the number of items.</p>\n<pre>t = 10, 20, 30        # packing: t is (10, 20, 30)\na, b, c = t           # unpacking\nprint(t, a, b, c)\nx, y = 1, 2\nx, y = y, x           # swap in one line!\nprint(x, y)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>(10, 20, 30) 10 20 30\n2 1</pre>\n<p><code>x, y = y, x</code> first packs <code>(y, x)</code> into a tuple, then unpacks it into <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>. That is the Python way to swap two variables.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>swap_pair(p)</code>, which is given a tuple <code>p</code> of two items and returns a new tuple with the two items swapped. Use unpacking.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>swap_pair((1, 2))</code> returns <code>(2, 1)</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def swap_pair(p):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(swap_pair((1, 2)))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(swap_pair((1, 2)))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "(2, 1)",
     "extra": "",
