  {
   "xml_index": 6,
   "xml_name": "L08_006_Returning Several Values",
   "display_name": "Returning Several Values",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>In Lab 4 you wrote functions like <code>return a, b</code> that return <b>several values</b>. Now you know what really happens: <code>return a, b</code> returns <b>one tuple</b> <code>(a, b)</code>, and <code>lo, hi = f(...)</code> unpacks it.</p>\n<pre>def divide(a, b):\n    return a // b, a % b\n\nr = divide(17, 5)\nprint(r, type(r))\nq, rem = divide(17, 5)\nprint(q, rem)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>(3, 2) &lt;class &#x27;tuple&#x27;&gt;\n3 2</pre>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>min_max(L)</code>, which returns a tuple <code>(smallest, largest)</code> for a non-empty list of integers <code>L</code>. (You may use <code>min()</code> and <code>max()</code>.)</p>\n<p>For example, <code>min_max([4, 1, 9, 7])</code> returns <code>(1, 9)</code>.</p>",
