   "xml_name": "L08_008_Nested Tuples",
   "display_name": "Nested Tuples",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Tuples can hold tuples. A classic use: a tuple of <b>points</b>, each point a tuple <code>(x, y)</code>:</p>\n<pre>points = ((0, 0), (3, 4), (6, 1))</pre>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">-1</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">points =</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">(0, 0)</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(52,129,188);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">(3, 4)</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">(6, 1)</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">1</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">2</td></tr></table><div style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a939c;margin:0 0 10px\">points[1] is (3, 4), and points[1][0] is 3</div>\n<pre>points = ((0, 0), (3, 4), (6, 1))\nprint(points[1])\nprint(points[1][0], points[1][1])\nfor x, y in points:\n    print(x + y)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>(3, 4)\n3 4\n0\n7\n7</pre>\n<p>The loop <code>for x, y in points:</code> <b>unpacks</b> each point into <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>total_x(points)</code>, which returns the sum of the x-coordinates (the first item) of all the points in the tuple <code>points</code>. For an empty tuple the sum is 0.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>total_x(((0, 0), (3, 4), (6, 1)))</code> returns <code>9</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def total_x(points):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(total_x(((0, 0), (3, 4), (6, 1))))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
