  {
   "xml_index": 9,
   "xml_name": "L08_009_Membership And Counting",
   "display_name": "Membership And Counting",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Tuples have only <b>two</b> methods, both for asking questions (neither changes anything):</p>\n<ul><li><code>t.count(x)</code>: how many items equal <code>x</code>;</li><li><code>t.index(x)</code>: the index of the first <code>x</code> (a <code>ValueError</code> if there is none).</li></ul>\n<p><code>x in t</code> and <code>len(t)</code> work as for lists.</p>\n<pre>t = (&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;, &quot;a&quot;, &quot;c&quot;)\nprint(&quot;a&quot; in t, &quot;z&quot; in t)\nprint(t.count(&quot;a&quot;), t.index(&quot;c&quot;))</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>True False\n2 3</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>describe(t, x)</code>, which returns a tuple of three values: whether <code>x</code> is in the tuple <code>t</code> (<code>True</code>/<code>False</code>), how many times it appears, and the index of its first appearance (or <code>-1</code> if it is not there).</p>\n<p>For example, <code>describe((\"a\", \"b\", \"a\"), \"a\")</code> returns <code>(True, 2, 0)</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def describe(t, x):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(describe((\"a\", \"b\", \"a\"), \"a\"))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
