  {
   "xml_index": 10,
   "xml_name": "L08_010_Loop With Unpacking",
   "display_name": "Loop With Unpacking",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A list of tuples is a common way to keep <b>records</b>, for example <code>(name, marks)</code> for each student. A <code>for</code> loop can unpack each record directly:</p>\n<pre>records = [(&quot;Asha&quot;, 82), (&quot;Ravi&quot;, 67), (&quot;Meera&quot;, 91)]\nfor name, marks in records:\n    print(name, &quot;scored&quot;, marks)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>Asha scored 82\nRavi scored 67\nMeera scored 91</pre>\n<p><b>Python fact:</b> <code>enumerate(seq)</code> gives pairs <code>(index, item)</code>, so <code>for i, item in enumerate(seq):</code> loops with the position too.</p>\n<pre>for i, c in enumerate((&quot;x&quot;, &quot;y&quot;, &quot;z&quot;)):\n    print(i, c)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>0 x\n1 y\n2 z</pre>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>names_above(records, cutoff)</code>, where <code>records</code> is a list of <code>(name, marks)</code> tuples. Return a list of the names whose marks are <b>more than</b> <code>cutoff</code>, in the original order.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>names_above([(\"Asha\", 82), (\"Ravi\", 67), (\"Meera\", 91)], 70)</code> returns <code>['Asha', 'Meera']</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def names_above(records, cutoff):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(names_above([(\"Asha\", 82), (\"Ravi\", 67), (\"Meera\", 91)], 70))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(names_above([(\"Asha\", 82), (\"Ravi\", 67), (\"Meera\", 91)], 70))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "['Asha', 'Meera']",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(names_above([(\"Asha\", 82)], 82))",
