  {
   "xml_index": 13,
   "xml_name": "L08_013_Strings Are Immutable Too",
   "display_name": "Strings Are Immutable Too",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Strings are immutable as well: <code>s[0] = \"x\"</code> is a <code>TypeError</code>. The same trick works: convert to a list of characters, change the list, and join the characters back into a string.</p>\n<p><b>Python fact:</b> <code>\"\".join(L)</code> joins a list (or tuple) of strings into one string, with nothing between them. <code>\"-\".join(L)</code> puts <code>-</code> between them.</p>\n<pre>word = &quot;hello&quot;\nL = list(word)\nL[0] = &quot;j&quot;\nprint(L)\nprint(&quot;&quot;.join(L))\nprint(&quot;-&quot;.join((&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;, &quot;c&quot;)))\nprint(word)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[&#x27;j&#x27;, &#x27;e&#x27;, &#x27;l&#x27;, &#x27;l&#x27;, &#x27;o&#x27;]\njello\na-b-c\nhello</pre>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>censor(word, ch)</code>, which returns a new string: <code>word</code> with every character equal to <code>ch</code> replaced by <code>\"*\"</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>censor(\"banana\", \"a\")</code> returns <code>\"b*n*n*\"</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define censor(word, ch): string -> list of characters -> change -> join.\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(censor(\"banana\", \"a\"))\n",
