  {
   "xml_index": 15,
   "xml_name": "L08_015_A List Inside A Tuple",
   "display_name": "A List Inside A Tuple",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Immutability has a subtle side. A tuple can <b>hold a list</b>. The tuple cannot change <b>which</b> objects it holds, but a list inside it is still a list, and <b>the list itself can change</b>:</p>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:collapse;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td><table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">record =</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(31,95,153);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;Asha&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(74,163,223);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#9679;</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">1</td></tr></table></td><td style=\"padding:0 8px\">&rarr;</td><td><table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(31,95,153);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">80</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(74,163,223);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">90</td></tr></table></td></tr></table><div style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a939c;margin:0 0 10px\">record[1] points to a list; the list can grow, but record[1] always points to the same list</div>\n<pre>record = (&quot;Asha&quot;, [80, 90])\nrecord[1].append(75)       # allowed: changes the list\nprint(record)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>(&#x27;Asha&#x27;, [80, 90, 75])</pre>\n<p>But <code>record[1] = [100]</code> is a <code>TypeError</code>: that would make the tuple hold a <b>different</b> object.</p>\n<p><b>Indentation reminder:</b> the body of an <code>if</code>, loop, function or class must be indented (press Tab or type 4 spaces). Only the spaces at the start of a line matter.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>add_mark(record, m)</code>, where <code>record</code> is a tuple <code>(name, marks)</code> and <code>marks</code> is a list. Add <code>m</code> to the end of the list of marks. The function returns nothing.</p>\n<p>For example, after <code>r = (\"Asha\", [80, 90])</code> and <code>add_mark(r, 75)</code>, <code>r</code> is <code>('Asha', [80, 90, 75])</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def add_mark(record, m):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# r = (\"Asha\", [80, 90])\n# add_mark(r, 75)\n# print(r)\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "r = (\"Asha\", [80, 90])\nadd_mark(r, 75)\nprint(r)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "('Asha', [80, 90, 75])",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "r = (\"Ravi\", [])\nadd_mark(r, 0)\nprint(r)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "('Ravi', [0])",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "r = (\"Meera\", [1])\nadd_mark(r, 2)\nadd_mark(r, 3)\nprint(r)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "('Meera', [1, 2, 3])",
     "extra": "",
