  {
   "xml_index": 17,
   "xml_name": "L08_017_Tuples Are Safe To Share",
   "display_name": "Tuples Are Safe To Share",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>In Lab 7 you saw <b>aliasing</b>: when a list is passed to a function, the function can change the caller's list, sometimes by accident. With a tuple this cannot happen: nobody can change it, so it is <b>safe to share</b>. This is one reason to use tuples for data that should stay fixed.</p>\n<pre>def add_zero_list(L):\n    L.append(0)            # changes the caller&#x27;s list!\n\ndef add_zero_tuple(t):\n    t = t + (0,)           # only a new tuple inside the function\n    return t\n\nnums = [1, 2]\nadd_zero_list(nums)\nprint(nums)\nfixed = (1, 2)\nprint(add_zero_tuple(fixed), fixed)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[1, 2, 0]\n(1, 2, 0) (1, 2)</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>with_extras(data, extra)</code>, where <code>data</code> is a tuple and <code>extra</code> is a list. Return a new tuple: the items of <code>data</code> followed by the items of <code>extra</code>. Neither <code>data</code> nor <code>extra</code> may change.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>with_extras((1, 2), [3, 4])</code> returns <code>(1, 2, 3, 4)</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define with_extras(data, extra): return a new tuple; change nothing.\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(with_extras((1, 2), [3, 4]))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(with_extras((1, 2), [3, 4]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "(1, 2, 3, 4)",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "d = (1,)\ne = []\nprint(with_extras(d, e), d, e)",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "(1,) (1,) []",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "e = list(map(int, input().split()))\nd = (0,)\nprint(with_extras(d, e), d, e)",
     "stdin": "5 6 7",
     "expected": "(0, 5, 6, 7) (0,) [5, 6, 7]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(with_extras((), ['a']))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "('a',)",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 18,
   "xml_name": "L08_018_Tuples As Fixed Records",
   "display_name": "Tuples As Fixed Records",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A point <code>(x, y)</code>, a date <code>(year, month, day)</code> or an RGB colour <code>(255, 128, 0)</code> has a <b>fixed number of parts</b> with fixed meanings. Tuples fit such <b>records</b> well. To \"move\" a point, we do not change it; we compute and return a <b>new</b> point.</p>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">p =</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(31,95,153);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(74,163,223);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">5</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">1</td></tr></table><div style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a939c;margin:0 0 10px\">move(p, 3, -1) returns the new point (5, 4); p is still (2, 5)</div>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>move(p, dx, dy)</code>, where <code>p</code> is a point <code>(x, y)</code>. Return the new point <code>(x + dx, y + dy)</code>.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>move((2, 5), 3, -1)</code> returns <code>(5, 4)</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define move(p, dx, dy): return a new point.\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(move((2, 5), 3, -1))\n",
