  {
   "xml_index": 20,
   "xml_name": "L08_020_Sorting A Tuple",
   "display_name": "Sorting A Tuple",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A tuple has no <code>sort()</code> method (it cannot change). The built-in <code>sorted(t)</code> works on a tuple, but it returns a <b>list</b>. Convert back with <code>tuple(...)</code> if you need a tuple:</p>\n<pre>t = (3, 1, 2)\ns = sorted(t)\nprint(s, type(s))\nprint(tuple(s))\nprint(tuple(sorted(t, reverse=True)))</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[1, 2, 3] &lt;class &#x27;list&#x27;&gt;\n(1, 2, 3)\n(3, 2, 1)</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>sorted_tuple(t)</code>, which returns a new tuple with the items of <code>t</code> sorted from small to large.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>sorted_tuple((3, 1, 2))</code> returns <code>(1, 2, 3)</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define sorted_tuple(t).\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(sorted_tuple((3, 1, 2)))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
