   "xml_index": 23,
   "xml_name": "L08_023_Topper From Records",
   "display_name": "Topper From Records",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Now combine what you know about tuples and lists. There is less help from here on: plan first, then test with small examples, including the corner cases mentioned.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>topper(records)</code>, where <code>records</code> is a non-empty list of <code>(name, marks)</code> tuples. Return the name with the highest marks; if several students share the highest marks, return the one that comes <b>first</b> in the list.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>topper([(\"Asha\", 82), (\"Ravi\", 91), (\"Meera\", 91)])</code> returns <code>\"Ravi\"</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define topper(records).\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(topper([(\"Asha\", 82), (\"Ravi\", 91), (\"Meera\", 91)]))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(topper([(\"Asha\", 82), (\"Ravi\", 91), (\"Meera\", 91)]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "Ravi",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(topper([(\"Solo\", 0)]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "Solo",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(topper([(\"A\", 5), (\"B\", 3), (\"C\", 5)]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "A",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(topper([(\"A\", -1), (\"B\", -3)]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "A",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "xml_index": 24,
   "xml_name": "L08_024_Pairing With Zip",
   "display_name": "Pairing With Zip",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><b>Python fact:</b> <code>zip(a, b)</code> pairs up the items of two sequences position by position, giving tuples. <code>list(zip(a, b))</code> shows them as a list of tuples:</p>\n<pre>names = [&quot;Asha&quot;, &quot;Ravi&quot;]\nmarks = [82, 67]\nprint(list(zip(names, marks)))\nfor n, m in zip(names, marks):\n    print(n, m)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[(&#x27;Asha&#x27;, 82), (&#x27;Ravi&#x27;, 67)]\nAsha 82\nRavi 67</pre>\n<p>Now combine what you know about tuples and lists. There is less help from here on: plan first, then test with small examples, including the corner cases mentioned.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write the function <code>pass_list(names, marks, cutoff)</code>, where <code>names</code> and <code>marks</code> are lists of the same length. Return a list of <code>(name, marks)</code> tuples for the students whose marks are at least <code>cutoff</code>, in the original order.</p>\n<p>For example, <code>pass_list([\"Asha\", \"Ravi\"], [82, 67], 70)</code> returns <code>[('Asha', 82)]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "# Define pass_list(names, marks, cutoff).\n\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(pass_list([\"Asha\", \"Ravi\"], [82, 67], 70))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(pass_list([\"Asha\", \"Ravi\"], [82, 67], 70))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[('Asha', 82)]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(pass_list([], [], 40))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(pass_list([\"A\", \"B\"], [40, 39], 40))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[('A', 40)]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
     "testcode": "print(pass_list([\"P\", \"Q\", \"R\"], [90, 90, 10], 50))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "[('P', 90), ('Q', 90)]",
     "extra": "",
     "show": false,
     "example": false,
     "in_precheck": false,
     "in_check": true
